# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Taxi API — environment (Phase 1)
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. NEVER commit a real .env.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# App
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000
# Iraq timezone — non-negotiable constant (see CLAUDE.md)
TZ=Asia/Baghdad

# Database — PostgreSQL (docker-compose uses postgis/postgis; PostGIS is
# available but unused in Phase 1, which stores plain lat/lng floats).
# Host port 5433 + DB name taxi_db match docker-compose.yml, which uses
# NON-default ports so this stack can run alongside another local stack (e.g.
# Aurora) without colliding. See docs/RUN_LOCAL.md.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://taxi:taxi@localhost:5433/taxi_db?schema=public"

# Cache / OTP store — Redis (host port 6380, also non-default; see above).
REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6380"

# Auth — JWT
JWT_SECRET="change-me-in-production-use-a-long-random-string"
JWT_EXPIRES_IN="30d"

# Legacy admin-by-phone seed. Kept so an ADMIN-role User still exists for the
# OTP path; the admin PANEL now uses the username/password accounts below.
ADMIN_PHONE=+9647700000000

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Admin panel — username + password (NOT WhatsApp OTP)
#
# Admins are a small internal group and are blocked on WhatsApp credentials,
# so they authenticate with a username and a password instead. They live in
# their own `AdminUser` table, separate from riders/drivers.
#
# `npm run prisma:seed` creates ONE SUPER_ADMIN from the two vars below.
# Its only extra privilege is managing other admin accounts.
#
# *** NEVER commit a real password. ***
# The seed hashes it with bcrypt and discards the plaintext; the password is
# never logged and is never returned by any endpoint. Re-running the seed does
# NOT overwrite an existing account's password — rotate it from the admin panel
# (or by deleting the row), not by editing this file.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SUPER_ADMIN_USERNAME=superadmin
SUPER_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-at-least-10-chars

# Admin sessions are shorter than the 30-day rider default: an admin token
# authorises pricing and driver approval, and it lives in a browser.
ADMIN_JWT_EXPIRES_IN="12h"

# Admin login brute-force limit, counted per username+IP. Only FAILED attempts
# count and a success clears the counter.
ADMIN_LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5
ADMIN_LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=900   # 15 min

# Driver document uploads — local disk in dev.
# TODO(prod): swap to object storage (S3-compatible) before production.
UPLOAD_DIR=./uploads

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No-show policy — rider reputation + temporary block
#
# A rider who books and does not turn up costs the driver a whole seat with no
# fare — 25% of a four-seat trip. Payments are cash-only in Phase 1, so there
# is no stored payment method to charge a fee against; the only lever we
# actually hold is access to the platform.
#
# THRESHOLD un-voided no-shows within WINDOW_DAYS  →  the rider cannot create
# NEW bookings for BLOCK_DAYS, counted from the LAST occurrence (not from the
# moment of counting — otherwise the block would renew itself on every check
# and never end). Existing confirmed bookings are never cancelled by a block.
#
# These are POLICY numbers, not engineering constants — the first real month of
# operation will change them, and an admin must be able to tune them without a
# code change. A missing or malformed value falls back to the default rather
# than failing boot: a typo in a policy knob does not deserve an outage.
# The rule itself lives in services/api/src/booking/no-show-policy.ts, and the
# appeal path (void one record / lift a block, with a reason) is the admin
# panel's /no-shows page. See docs/PHASE1_BUILD_BRIEF.md → `no-show`.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NO_SHOW_BLOCK_THRESHOLD=3     # occurrences that trigger a block
NO_SHOW_WINDOW_DAYS=30        # rolling window they must fall within
NO_SHOW_BLOCK_DAYS=7          # cooling-off length, from the last occurrence

# ── Route requests («أبلغنا أنك تريد هذا المسار») ──────────────
# How long a rider's request for an unserved corridor stays live: inside this
# window it can still trigger a notification when a driver posts, and it is
# counted on the admin's route-requests screen. Outside it, neither.
#
# It is a window rather than a lifetime for two reasons. A rider asked in
# February does not want a ping in May about a trip they have forgotten; and
# the COUNT is the whole value of the feature — an unbounded total only grows
# and says nothing about demand *now*, which is the only question that decides
# where to recruit a driver this week.
#
# Malformed or non-positive falls back to the default, same as the no-show
# knobs. Rule: services/api/src/trip/route-request-policy.ts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ROUTE_REQUEST_TTL_DAYS=30     # a request stops counting (and notifying) after this

# ── Phase 2: pooling (rider-initiated trips) ───────────────────
# A rider asks for a seat on a corridor inside a time window; compatible
# requests pool; a driver claims the assembled pool from a board and it
# becomes an ordinary Trip (createdBy=SYSTEM).
#
# POOL_MAX_SEATS   the largest vehicle we support. A pool bigger than any car
#                  we have is not a pool, it is trapped requests.
# POOL_MIN_SEATS   what makes a pool worth driving. Governs TWO things on
#                  purpose — when a pool appears on the board, and how many
#                  seats must accept after a price raise — because they are
#                  one question ("is this trip worth driving?") and two
#                  thresholds for it would drift apart.
# POOL_RAISE_BLACKOUT_MINUTES  no price raise may be proposed inside this much
#                  of the window's START: a rider who declines needs time to
#                  make other plans.
# POOL_RAISE_RESPONSE_MINUTES  how long a rider has to answer. NO ANSWER IS A
#                  DECLINE. Must stay SHORTER than the blackout, or the
#                  deadline could fall after the car has left (the code clamps
#                  it to departure as a backstop).
# POOL_MAX_WINDOW_HOURS  widest window one request may ask for. An unbounded
#                  window overlaps everything and pools people who share
#                  nothing real.
#
# Malformed or non-positive falls back to the default, same as the other
# policy knobs. Rule: services/api/src/pool/pool-policy.ts.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
POOL_MAX_SEATS=4
POOL_MIN_SEATS=2
POOL_RAISE_BLACKOUT_MINUTES=30
POOL_RAISE_RESPONSE_MINUTES=10
POOL_MAX_WINDOW_HOURS=6

# OTP policy
OTP_TTL_SECONDS=300           # how long a code stays valid (5 min)
OTP_LENGTH=6
OTP_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=3          # max request-otp per window, per phone
OTP_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS=600   # window = 10 min
OTP_MAX_VERIFY_ATTEMPTS=5     # wrong-code attempts before a code is burned

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WhatsApp Business Cloud API (OTP delivery)
# Reuse the Sehat Beitak setup. If ANY of the three vars below are
# missing, the API falls back to logging the OTP to the console
# (DEV-ONLY). That fallback is a HARD release blocker and is refused
# when NODE_ENV=production.
#
# LOCAL DEV (no WhatsApp yet): leave these three EMPTY. The server then
# prints the login code to its logs, e.g.:
#   [DEV-ONLY][RELEASE BLOCKER] WhatsApp not configured — OTP for +9647… is 123456.
# There is no separate OTP_DEV_LOG flag — "empty WhatsApp config" IS the switch.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
WHATSAPP_OTP_TEMPLATE=
WHATSAPP_TEMPLATE_LANG=ar
WHATSAPP_API_VERSION=v21.0

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Push notifications — FCM (Firebase Admin SDK).
# If any of the three below are missing, notifications are logged to the
# console instead (DEV-ONLY — not a release blocker). Set all three in prod.
# FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY: paste the key with literal \n escapes (they're
# converted to real newlines at boot).
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
FIREBASE_CLIENT_EMAIL=
FIREBASE_PRIVATE_KEY=
